  "image_name": "3b20fab96f144c10b6c5c4740e9c3076.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure illustrates how maternal stress can suppress immunity through the hypothalamic–pituitary–adrenal axis. Stressors such as LPS or poor maternal nutrition are shown activating the hypothalamus, which releases CRF and stimulates the pituitary to produce ACTH. That signal then acts on the adrenal glands, increasing corticosteroid production.\n\nThe elevated corticosteroids are presented as the central mediator linking stress to multiple downstream effects in immune and digestive organs. Arrows connect them to the spleen and stomach, and to a box labeled immunosuppression, indicating weakened immune function and impaired intestinal function. The figure emphasizes that this immune suppression contributes to poor maternal resistance to disease and worsening gut function.\n\nA further consequence appears in the bone marrow and the fetus. The diagram suggests that stress-related immune changes affect bone marrow function and reduce maternal antibody transfer to offspring through the placenta and milk. Overall, the image conveys a chain of events in which maternal stress hormones broadly weaken maternal immunity and indirectly limit immune protection passed to the developing or newborn offspring.",
